What was deliberately left out, and why
---------------------------------------

    serve/              the upstream Python serving layer. Q-Infer keeps its own HTTP
                        layer (OpenAI + Anthropic APIs, web UI, telemetry) and drives
                        the engine over its stdin/stdout line protocol instead.
    sycl/               the experimental Intel Arc / SYCL port (its own CMake project,
                        requires icpx + oneMKL). We build CUDA only.
    tests/, bench/      upstream's test and microbenchmark harness. Omitting them also
                        makes STRATA_BUILD_TESTS default to OFF, per the guard in the
                        upstream CMakeLists.txt (line 95): the option only turns ON when
                        both directories exist.
    setup.py, setup.sh  the pack preprocessing step. It is a development-time input, not
                        something we build into the serving binary.
    docs/, data/, tools/, chat.py, ref/, Dockerfile, *.bat, READMEs, .github/
                        documentation, model artifacts and release plumbing. Model
                        weights must never enter this repository in any case.

How it is used
--------------

The vendored engine is configured and built by `tools/build_engine.sh` into
`build-engine/strata`, a separate binary with its own heavy CUDA toolchain
dependency. Q-Infer's serving binary never links it; it talks to the engine as a
subprocess. That process boundary is what keeps our "zero runtime dependencies"
rule intact — see `docs/design/adr/ADR-016-vendored-strata-backend.md`, and
`docs/design/adr/ADR-008-*` for the dependency rule itself.

Q-Infer's own code is MIT (c) 2026 crazymelo1005, which is compatible with the
upstream terms above.

Build note
----------

`tools/build_engine.sh` does not configure `third_party/strata/` in place. It copies the tree to
`build-engine/src/` and configures from there, because the CUDA section of the upstream
CMakeLists.txt registers one target (`dma_batch_parity`, line 807) from `tests/core/` without a
`STRATA_BUILD_TESTS` guard, and CMake resolves every `add_executable` while configuring — so a
partial source tree cannot be configured as it stands. Upstream guards eight other partial-tree
cases the same way with `AND EXISTS` (lines 482, 492, 499, 526, 534, 544, 549, 554); line 807 reads
as an oversight. The copy receives a placeholder at that path which fails loudly if anything ever
tries to compile it, so the vendored tree itself is never edited and stays byte-identical to
`a0116b27`. The script prints a confirmation of that at the end of every build.

----------

Test data (P2): `tests/data/chat_golden.json` and `tests/data/chat_template.jinja` are copies
from the same upstream checkout (`serve/`), used as the byte-parity oracle for our chat
renderer (`tests/test_chat_golden.cpp`). They are data, not code; the renderer is ours.

----------

Vision encoder (M5a): `tools/vision/qinfer_vision.cpp` and `tools/vision/CMakeLists.txt` are a
port of the same two files from Strata `tools/vision` (same MIT copyright holder as above),
tag `v0.1.43.1`. `measure/vision_port_check.py` derives them from that tag and lists every
allowed divergence; the stdout line protocol and the SVE1 byte layout are upstream's verbatim.

That encoder links llama.cpp, which is NOT in this repository. `tools/build_vision.sh` extracts
commit 3cf03257f219afbe7334045ff7c6a06ac68c627d (llama.cpp, MIT) from a local checkout with
`git archive` into the git-ignored `build-vision/llama.cpp/`. It is never downloaded and never
vendored, and the built encoder is spawned by the serving layer rather than linked into
`qinfer-server`, so the zero-runtime-dependency rule still holds (ADR-013, ADR-008).

